    (v_topic_id, 'A 55-old female presents with sudden, excruciating pain in the left eye, accompanied by a severe unilateral headache, nausea, and two episodes of vomiting over the last 2 hours. She reports seeing rainbow-colored halos around lights and experiencing rapidly progressive blurry vision. Physical examination of the left eye reveals:<br>• Visual acuity: <b>20/200</b><br>• Conjunctival injection: <b>Moderate-to-severe ciliary flush</b><br>• Cornea: <b>Hazy and edematous</b><br>• Pupil: <b>Mid-dilated, fixed, and non-reactive</b><br>• Intraocular pressure (IOP): <b>56 mmHg</b> (normal: 10-21 mmHg)<br><br>What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Herpes simplex virus keratitis", "Neovascular glaucoma secondary to proliferative diabetic retinopathy", "Acute anterior uveitis", "Central retinal artery occlusion", "Acute angle-closure glaucoma"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This patient presents with a classic, textbook emergency scenario of **acute angle-closure glaucoma (AACG)**. AACG occurs when the iridocorneal angle narrows, leading to a pupillary block that completely obstructs aqueous humor outflow. The sudden accumulation of aqueous humor causes a rapid, dramatic spike in intraocular pressure (IOP), often exceeding 40-50 mmHg. The clinical hallmark is the combination of intense ocular pain, ipsilateral headache, nausea/vomiting, blurry vision with colored halos, a ''steamy'' or hazy cornea, ciliary injection, and a **fixed mid-dilated pupil** (due to sphincter muscle ischemia). Definitive treatment is a bilateral laser peripheral iridotomy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-old gentleman is diagnosed with acute angle-closure glaucoma in the left eye. The measured intraocular pressure is <b>47 mmHg</b>. While preparing the patient for definitive laser peripheral iridotomy, you must initiate urgent medical therapy to rapidly lower the intraocular pressure. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial medical regimen and sequence?', '["Topical beta-blocker (Timolol), topical alpha-2 agonist (Apraclonidine), systemic carbonic anhydrase inhibitor (Acetazolamide), followed by topical Pilocarpine once IOP decreases", "Topical atropine drops to dilate the pupil, combined with intravenous methylprednisolone", "Intravenous heparin bolus followed by immediate surgical anterior chamber paracentesis", "Oral Latanoprost drops every 2 hours, combined with topical homatropine to relieve ciliary muscle spasm", "Topical Dorzolamide mono-therapy without systemic medication to avoid metabolic acidosis risk"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The immediate medical management of acute angle-closure glaucoma involves multi-mechanism pressure reduction to salvage the optic nerve. Topical **Timolol** (beta-blocker) and **Apraclonidine** (alpha-2 agonist) decrease aqueous production. Systemic **Acetazolamide** (carbonic anhydrase inhibitor) drastically reduces active transport of bicarbonate into the posterior chamber. **Pilocarpine** (a miotic agent) is highly effective at pulling the peripheral iris away from the trabecular meshwork; however, it must **not** be administered immediately if the IOP is extremely high (usually >40-50 mmHg) because the iris sphincter is ischemic and unresponsive. Once IOP drops slightly below 40 mmHg, pilocarpine should be given. Definitive treatment remains laser peripheral iridotomy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-old male is referred to an ophthalmology clinic after a routine optometrist exam. The patient has no visual complaints and denies eye pain, redness, or headaches. He has a medical history of hypertension and a maternal family history of blindness. Automated visual field testing reveals bilateral arcuate scotomas. Slit-lamp biomicroscopy of both eyes demonstrates:<br>• Anterior chamber angles: <b>Open and normal-appearing (Grade 4)</b><br>• Corneas: <b>Clear, normal thickness</b><br>• Optic discs: <b>Increased cup-to-disc ratio of 0.75</b> with asymmetric thinning of the neuroretinal rim<br>• Intraocular pressure (IOP): <b>27 mmHg</b> in both eyes<br><br>What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Normal-tension glaucoma (NTG)", "Primary open-angle glaucoma (POAG)", "Optic neuritis secondary to Multiple Sclerosis", "Ocular hypertension without glaucomatous damage", "Chronic angle-closure glaucoma"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This clinical picture represents classic **Primary Open-Angle Glaucoma (POAG)**. POAG is characterized by an insidious, painless, progressive loss of retinal ganglion cells, leading to optic nerve cupping (increased **cup-to-disc ratio**) and characteristic visual field defects (like **arcuate or nasal scotomas**, eventually progressing to tunnel vision). The anterior chamber angle remains clinically open and normal-appearing on gonioscopy, and the IOP is chronically elevated (typically >21 mmHg). Risk factors include advanced age, black race, family history, and diabetes.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 38-old man with newly diagnosed primary open-angle glaucoma is started on first-line pharmacotherapy with Latanoprost ophthalmic solution (one drop daily at bedtime). The patient asks how this medication works to lower the pressure in his eyes. What is the primary mechanism of action of Latanoprost?', '["Decreasing active sodium transport into the posterior chamber by inhibiting Na+/K+ ATPase", "Decreasing aqueous production by inhibiting carbonic anhydrase in the ciliary body processes", "Decreasing aqueous humor production by blocking beta-2 receptors on the ciliary body epithelium", "Increasing aqueous humor drainage through the uveoscleral outflow pathway", "Increasing drainage through the trabecular meshwork via ciliary muscle contraction"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Prostaglandin analogs (such as **Latanoprost**, Bimatoprost, and Travoprost) are the preferred first-line medical therapy for primary open-angle glaucoma due to their excellent once-daily efficacy, flat 24-hour diurnal control, and favorable systemic safety profile. They work by **increasing aqueous humor drainage through the uveoscleral outflow pathway** (remodeling extracellular matrix in the ciliary muscle bundle). Timolol, a beta-blocker, works by decreasing aqueous production. Pilocarpine, a cholinergic agonist, increases drainage via the trabecular meshwork by contracting the ciliary muscle.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 35-old woman is evaluated for progressive primary open-angle glaucoma. The physician considers adding Timolol 0.5% ophthalmic drops to the patient''s regimen. However, upon reviewing the patient''s chart, which of the following medical histories would serve as a strict contraindication to the initiation of topical Timolol therapy?', '["Moderate-to-severe asthma or advanced chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)", "History of severe sulfa allergy or G6PD deficiency", "Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) with chronic urinary retention", "Closed-angle glaucoma history treated with laser iridotomy", "Chronic kidney disease Stage III with an eGFR of 45 mL/min"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Topical beta-blockers like **Timolol** are highly effective at lowering intraocular pressure by **decreasing aqueous humor production** at the ciliary body epithelium. However, they undergo significant systemic absorption through the nasolacrimal duct (bypassing first-pass hepatic metabolism). This can lead to classic systemic beta-blocker adverse effects, including **bronchospasm**, bradycardia, heart block, and worsening of congestive heart failure. Therefore, they are strictly contraindicated in patients with **active asthma, reactive airway disease, advanced COPD, sinus bradycardia, or second- or third-degree heart block**.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 75-old myopic man presents with intermittent blurry vision and halos around lights, which classically occur after strenuous treadmill exercise. Gonioscopy shows a widely open angle with dense, dark brown pigment deposition in the trabecular meshwork (a homogeneous pigment band). Slit-lamp biomicroscopy reveals:<br>• Cornea: <b>Vertical pigment deposition on the corneal endothelium (Krukenberg spindle)</b><br>• Iris: <b>Slit-like, radial transillumination defects</b><br>• Intraocular pressure: <b>29 mmHg</b> (post-exercise: 56 mmHg)<br><br>What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Pseudoexfoliation glaucoma", "Neovascular glaucoma", "Acute angle-closure glaucoma", "Fuchs endothelial corneal dystrophy", "Pigmentary glaucoma / Pigment dispersion syndrome"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This classic clinical vignette describes **pigmentary glaucoma**, a form of secondary open-angle glaucoma. It classically occurs in **young, myopic males** due to a mechanical abnormality where a concave iris rubs against the lens zonules during pupillary movements. This causes the shedding of iris pigment, which accumulates in the trabecular meshwork (obstructing outflow) and deposits vertically on the corneal endothelium (**Krukenberg spindle**). The classic diagnostic features are radial **iris transillumination defects**, an open angle with a dark pigment band on gonioscopy, and **spikes of IOP associated with exercise or pupillary dilation** (pigment release storms).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 54-old gentleman is scheduled to undergo cataract extraction. During the preoperative slit-lamp examination, the ophthalmologist notes greyish-white, dandruff-like fibrillar material deposited on the anterior lens capsule in a target-like pattern, as well as on the pupillary margin. The intraocular pressure is <b>28 mmHg</b>, and there is evidence of glaucomatous optic nerve cupping. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis, and what intraoperative complication must the surgeon prepare for?', '["Congenital glaucoma; scleral ectasia and vitreous loss during entry incision", "Pigmentary glaucoma; severe intraoperative hemorrhage from iris neovascularization", "Primary open-angle glaucoma; corneal endothelial decompensation and Descemet rupture", "Pseudoexfoliation glaucoma; intraoperative zonular dialysis and lens subluxation", "Steroid-induced glaucoma; posterior capsular rupture due to dense fibrous plaques"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Pseudoexfoliation glaucoma** is the most common cause of secondary open-angle glaucoma worldwide. It is characterized by the deposition of an abnormal fibrillar protein material throughout the anterior segment (pupillary border, anterior lens capsule in a **target-like central disc and peripheral band**, and trabecular meshwork). Crucially, this material also deposits on the **ocular zonules**, leading to progressive **zonular weakness, instability, and a high risk of zonular dialysis, vitreous loss, or lens subluxation/dislocation during cataract surgery**.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-old woman has documented progressive cupping of the optic nerve head (cup-to-disc ratio: 0.75) and corresponding arcuate visual field defects. The anterior chamber angle is open and normal-appearing. Repeated diurnal intraocular pressure (IOP) readings range from <b>14 to 17 mmHg</b> (consistently below 21 mmHg). The patient also has a history of severe migraines and cold hands (Raynaud''s phenomenon). What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy (AION)", "Ischemic optic neuropathy", "Primary open-angle glaucoma (POAG)", "Ocular hypertension", "Normal-tension glaucoma (NTG)"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Normal-Tension Glaucoma (NTG)** is a variant of primary open-angle glaucoma where patients display classic glaucomatous optic neuropathy (cupping, thinning of the neuroretinal rim, visual field loss) in the setting of **consistently normal intraocular pressures (<= 21 mmHg)**. NTG is strongly associated with **vasospastic disorders** (such as migraines, Raynaud''s phenomenon, and sleep apnea) and systemic nocturnal hypotension (frequently exacerbated by taking antihypertensive medications at bedtime), indicating a primary vascular perfusion issue of the optic nerve head.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 71-old male with a history of poorly controlled type 2 diabetes and a recent central retinal vein occlusion (CRVO) presents with pain and redness in the left eye. Slit-lamp biomicroscopy reveals small, abnormal, branching blood vessels on the surface of the pupillary border (rubeosis iridis) and extending into the iridocorneal angle. The intraocular pressure is <b>47 mmHg</b>. What is the underlying pathogen and the most appropriate management strategy for this condition?', '["Autoimmune ciliary body inflammation; treatment with high-dose systemic corticosteroids", "Zonular weakness with lens displacement; treatment with immediate lens extraction", "Fibrillar protein outflow obstruction; treatment with selective laser trabeculoplasty (SLT)", "Mechanical pupillary block; treatment with immediate laser peripheral iridotomy", "VEGF-driven angle closure; treatment with anti-VEGF injections and panretinal photocoagulation (PRP)"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Neovascular Glaucoma (NVG)** is a severe form of secondary glaucoma triggered by retinal ischemia (most commonly due to **diabetic retinopathy or central retinal vein occlusion**). Retinal ischemia stimulates the release of vascular endothelial growth factor (**VEGF**), which diffuses to the anterior segment, causing **neovascularization of the iris (rubeosis iridis)** and the iridocorneal angle. The new vessels and associated fibrous membrane contract, mechanically pulling the iris over the trabecular meshwork (synechial angle closure). Treatment requires reducing ischemia via **panretinal photocoagulation (PRP)** and/or **anti-VEGF injections**, combined with aggressive IOP-lowering therapy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A parents of a 13-month-old infant bring his to the pediatric ophthalmology clinic because they noticed the child''s eyes look unusually large, and he constantly tears (epiphora), squints in bright light (photophobia), and keeps his eyelids tightly closed (blepharospasm). Physical examination under anesthesia reveals:<br>• Corneas: <b>Enlarged (diameter > 12 mm) and cloudy due to epithelial edema</b><br>• Optic discs: <b>Significant cupping</b><br>• Intraocular pressure: <b>28 mmHg</b><br><br>What is the most likely diagnosis, and what is the definitive management?', '["Congenital dacryostenosis; nasolacrimal duct probing and massage", "Ophthalmia neonatorum; intravenous Ceftriaxone injection", "Congenital cataracts; lens aspiration and intraocular lens placement", "Dacryocystitis; outpatient topical and oral antibiotic therapy", "Primary congenital glaucoma; surgical intervention (goniotomy or trabeculotomy)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This infant presents with the classic triad of **primary congenital glaucoma**: **epiphora (tearing), photophobia, and blepharospasm**. Congenital glaucoma results from abnormal development of the anterior chamber angle (specifically trabeculodysgenesis or persistence of Barkan''s membrane). Because the infant sclera is highly elastic, elevated intraocular pressure leads to progressive stretching and enlargement of the globe (**buphthalmos** or ''ox-eye'') and enlarged corneal diameter (>12 mm). Corneal edema occurs due to endothelial stretch and fluid entry. **Definitive treatment is surgical** (goniotomy or trabeculotomy) to restore drainage; medical management is only a temporary bridge.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 47-old man presents with sudden, excruciating pain in the left eye, accompanied by a severe unilateral headache, nausea, and two episodes of vomiting over the last 2 hours. He reports seeing rainbow-colored halos around lights and experiencing rapidly progressive blurry vision. Physical examination of the left eye reveals:<br>• Visual acuity: <b>Count fingers at 3 feet</b><br>• Conjunctival injection: <b>Moderate-to-severe ciliary flush</b><br>• Cornea: <b>Hazy and edematous</b><br>• Pupil: <b>Mid-dilated, fixed, and non-reactive</b><br>• Intraocular pressure (IOP): <b>60 mmHg</b> (normal: 10-21 mmHg)<br><br>What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Neovascular glaucoma secondary to proliferative diabetic retinopathy", "Acute anterior uveitis", "Herpes simplex virus keratitis", "Central retinal artery occlusion", "Acute angle-closure glaucoma"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This patient presents with a classic, textbook emergency scenario of **acute angle-closure glaucoma (AACG)**. AACG occurs when the iridocorneal angle narrows, leading to a pupillary block that completely obstructs aqueous humor outflow. The sudden accumulation of aqueous humor causes a rapid, dramatic spike in intraocular pressure (IOP), often exceeding 40-50 mmHg. The clinical hallmark is the combination of intense ocular pain, ipsilateral headache, nausea/vomiting, blurry vision with colored halos, a ''steamy'' or hazy cornea, ciliary injection, and a **fixed mid-dilated pupil** (due to sphincter muscle ischemia). Definitive treatment is a bilateral laser peripheral iridotomy.'),
